WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.433
Training extras, part two.

2
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:07.533
Eight more terms: training at scale, data, and a few more methods worth recognizing.

3
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:12.533
DeepSpeed and FSDP are distributed training engines.

4
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:18.999
They shard model states across GPUs, using ZeRO stages or fully-sharded data parallel,

5
00:00:18.999 --> 00:00:21.867
so models bigger than one GPU can train.

6
00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:25.633
They're mentioned in every train-big-models job description.

7
00:00:26.833 --> 00:00:31.132
Synthetic data is training data generated by a stronger LLM:

8
00:00:31.132 --> 00:00:34.500
instructions, preferences, or reasoning traces.

9
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.400
Most modern fine-tunes are largely synthetic, so quality-filtering it is the real skill.

10
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:47.500
Data contamination is when benchmark questions leak into the training data, inflating scores.

11
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:53.067
It's why public benchmark claims get side-eye, and why your own private benchmark matters.

12
00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:55.767
Two data strategies here.

13
00:00:56.067 --> 00:00:59.333
Curriculum orders the training data from easy to hard.

14
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:04.859
Active learning lets the model pick which unlabeled examples are worth labeling next,

15
00:01:04.859 --> 00:01:07.133
which maximizes your labeling budget.

16
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:13.282
Continual pretraining keeps running the pretraining objective on domain text,

17
00:01:13.282 --> 00:01:16.367
like legal corpora or your industry's documents.

18
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:22.000
You do it before SFT, for domains whose vocabulary the base model barely saw.

19
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.296
These are other parameter-efficient methods.

20
00:01:26.296 --> 00:01:29.533
AdaLoRA and P-tuning ship in the peft library.

21
00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:31.900
AdaLoRA adapts ranks per layer.

22
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.333
P-tuning learns soft prompt vectors.

23
00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:39.023
BitFit needs no library: you freeze everything except the biases.

24
00:01:39.023 --> 00:01:42.400
LoRA won the war, so just recognize the also-rans.

25
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.311
In self-play, a model improves by competing against or judging itself,

26
00:01:48.311 --> 00:01:50.667
generating its own training signal.

27
00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:56.933
It's the AlphaGo idea, now reappearing in LLM reasoning training and synthetic-data loops.

28
00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:04.200
Data mixing is the proportion of code, web text, math, and multilingual data in pretraining.

29
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.870
It's a quiet but decisive driver of a model's character,

30
00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:11.300
which is why some models code well and others write well.

31
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:13.467
Let's recap.

32
00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:18.367
DeepSpeed and FSDP shard big training jobs across GPUs.

33
00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:24.133
Synthetic data needs quality filtering, and contamination inflates benchmark scores.

34
00:02:24.433 --> 00:02:27.433
And data mixing quietly shapes a model's character.
